WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.973 --> 00:00:18.535
Autism is something
that many people know about.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.890
For example, some people think

00:00:21.914 --> 00:00:26.440
that autistic people
are fair-skinned males

00:00:26.464 --> 00:00:28.519
that speak in monotone

00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:32.130
and constantly go on and on
about the same topic.

00:00:33.258 --> 00:00:38.252
Some people think that autistic people
do not know right from wrong,

00:00:38.276 --> 00:00:39.808
avoid attention

00:00:39.832 --> 00:00:43.832
and usually say the wrong thing
at the wrong time.

00:00:43.856 --> 00:00:48.354
Some people think that autistic people
are socially awkward

00:00:48.378 --> 00:00:50.417
and lack humor and empathy.

00:00:51.679 --> 00:00:55.060
Now if you agree with what I just said,

00:00:55.084 --> 00:00:56.498
I'm sorry to tell you,

00:00:56.522 --> 00:01:00.307
but you do not have
the right impression of autism.

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:02.084
How do I know?

00:01:02.744 --> 00:01:05.593
Because I have autism.

00:01:06.323 --> 00:01:11.437
I do have my own obsessions
with things like electronics

00:01:11.461 --> 00:01:13.736
and public transit,

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.188
but that does not define me.

00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:20.378
Each of us are different
and unique in our own way.

00:01:21.252 --> 00:01:24.839
However, there is not
a lot of information out there

00:01:24.863 --> 00:01:28.069
on what an autistic life
actually looks like,

00:01:28.093 --> 00:01:31.458
so people often resort to stereotypes.

00:01:31.831 --> 00:01:34.231
And we see these often in the media.

00:01:34.712 --> 00:01:38.744
Some of the more common
stereotypes in the media

00:01:38.768 --> 00:01:40.910
include being socially awkward,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.633
lacking empathy

00:01:42.657 --> 00:01:45.164
and even being a supergenius.

00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:52.503
And the lack of knowledge
on autism doesn't stop there either.

00:01:52.527 --> 00:01:56.125
Did you know that some people
are trying to find a cure for autism?

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.752
That's because they see it
as a negative thing,

00:01:58.776 --> 00:02:00.291
as a disease.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.410
Many people are challenging the idea

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:07.990
and to us, we think autism
is not a disease.

00:02:08.014 --> 00:02:12.188
It's just another way of thinking
and looking at the world.

00:02:12.679 --> 00:02:16.188
Our brains function differently
from most people's brains.

00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:19.926
Think of it like comparing
and Xbox and a PlayStation.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.490
They're both highly capable consoles
with different programming.

00:02:24.292 --> 00:02:28.188
But if you put your Xbox game
in a PlayStation,

00:02:28.212 --> 00:02:32.521
it won't work, because the PlayStation
communicates differently.

00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:38.588
When I look in the mirror,

00:02:38.612 --> 00:02:40.706
I see someone who thinks differently.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.603
Oh, and I also see nice hair.

00:02:42.627 --> 00:02:44.665
(Laughter)

00:02:44.689 --> 00:02:51.313
(Applause)

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.448
But the question is,

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:56.805
am I really diseased
if I just think differently?

00:03:00.387 --> 00:03:05.061
The main problem with living autistic
in today's society

00:03:05.085 --> 00:03:08.077
is that the world just isn't built for us.

00:03:08.101 --> 00:03:11.652
There's so many ways
that we can get overwhelmed.

00:03:12.478 --> 00:03:14.057
For example,

00:03:14.081 --> 00:03:19.355
the thing that makes me overwhelmed
all the time is loud noises,

00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:22.478
which means I never
crank up my music really loud

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:26.601
and I usually am not a fan
of large parties.

00:03:27.323 --> 00:03:30.762
But other people on the spectrum
might get overwhelmed

00:03:30.786 --> 00:03:34.260
with things like bright lights
or strong smells

00:03:34.284 --> 00:03:36.307
or gooey textures

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.217
that all have the potential
to create anxiety.

00:03:40.847 --> 00:03:46.299
Think about all of the social gatherings
you've been to in the past.

00:03:46.323 --> 00:03:48.257
Was there loud music playing?

00:03:48.807 --> 00:03:51.394
Were there really bright lights?

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.236
Were there lots of different food smells
going on at the same time?

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:00.243
Were there lots of conversations
happening all at once?

00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:03.959
Those things may not
have bothered you guys,

00:04:03.983 --> 00:04:06.855
but for someone with autism,

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.946
they can be quite overwhelming.

00:04:09.522 --> 00:04:15.156
So in those situations,
we do something called stimming,

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.230
which is like a repetitive
motion or a noise

00:04:18.254 --> 00:04:23.822
or some other random fidgeting
that may or may not seem normal.

00:04:24.853 --> 00:04:27.242
Some people will flap their arms

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:30.860
or make a noise or spin.

00:04:32.361 --> 00:04:35.479
Ya, it's basically our way of zoning out.

00:04:36.559 --> 00:04:39.432
It can often feel necessary
for us to stim.

00:04:39.893 --> 00:04:43.043
However, it's often frowned upon,

00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:44.934
and we're forced to hide it.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.881
When we're forced to hide
our autistic traits like this,

00:04:49.905 --> 00:04:51.239
it's called masking.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.491
And some people mask better than others.

00:04:56.865 --> 00:05:02.712
I mask so well sometimes
that people don't even know I'm autistic

00:05:02.736 --> 00:05:06.546
until I give them the big reveal. (Laughs)

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.620
But at the end of the day,
it gets really stressful.

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:14.882
Even something like
doing my homework at night

00:05:14.906 --> 00:05:16.771
becomes very tiring.

00:05:17.839 --> 00:05:19.902
Some people think,

00:05:21.347 --> 00:05:23.434
because of our ability to mask,

00:05:23.458 --> 00:05:25.592
that this is the cure to autism.

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:28.721
However, all it really does
is makes us ashamed

00:05:28.745 --> 00:05:31.038
of showing our true selves.

00:05:35.839 --> 00:05:40.950
Another common stereotype
that is often associated with autism

00:05:40.974 --> 00:05:43.441
is that autistic people lack empathy.

00:05:44.458 --> 00:05:46.497
And again, this is not true.

00:05:47.124 --> 00:05:49.258
I actually have lots of empathy.

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:52.201
I'm just not really good at showing it.

00:05:52.997 --> 00:05:55.152
Whenever a friend is trying to tell me

00:05:55.176 --> 00:05:57.843
some of the struggles
that they're going through,

00:05:57.867 --> 00:06:00.914
I often don't know
how to express my reply.

00:06:01.271 --> 00:06:03.597
And that is why I don't show
as much empathy

00:06:03.621 --> 00:06:06.080
as my nonautistic friends do.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:13.324
Emotional expression,
however much or however little,

00:06:13.348 --> 00:06:14.710
is difficult for me.

00:06:15.671 --> 00:06:18.403
And that is because I am bursting inside

00:06:18.427 --> 00:06:22.442
with every single emotion
one feels at all times.

00:06:23.188 --> 00:06:26.498
Though of course,
I cannot express it that way.

00:06:26.522 --> 00:06:31.052
Otherwise, let's say,
happiness, for example,

00:06:31.076 --> 00:06:34.354
would come out as a huge burst
of gleeful wheezing,

00:06:34.378 --> 00:06:37.814
hand flapping and loud vocal "woohoos."

00:06:37.838 --> 00:06:39.377
(Laughter)

00:06:40.125 --> 00:06:41.776
Whereas you may just smile.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.126
(Laughter)

00:06:45.569 --> 00:06:49.761
Whether it be receiving
an awesome birthday gift

00:06:49.785 --> 00:06:55.111
or listening to a tragic
story on the news,

00:06:55.135 --> 00:06:59.895
I cannot really express my reply
without bursting,

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:04.906
so once again, I have to mask it
in order to appear normal.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:08.855
My inner feelings are unlimited,

00:07:08.879 --> 00:07:13.136
but my mind only lets me
express extremes or nothing.

00:07:15.804 --> 00:07:17.836
So my ...

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:21.517
I am not great with my emotions,

00:07:21.541 --> 00:07:24.569
and I communicate differently,

00:07:24.593 --> 00:07:29.815
and because of that, I was diagnosed
with autism spectrum disorder.

00:07:30.514 --> 00:07:34.915
This diagnosis helps me
and my friends and family

00:07:34.939 --> 00:07:38.447
to know how my mind works.

00:07:39.511 --> 00:07:40.925
And in the world,

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:43.564
approximately one percent
of the population

00:07:43.588 --> 00:07:47.164
is diagnosed with autism
spectrum disorder.

00:07:47.188 --> 00:07:48.988
And this number is growing.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.852
However, we are still a big minority.

00:07:53.217 --> 00:07:56.876
And there's still lots of people
that do not see us as equals

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:58.209
to other people.

00:08:00.368 --> 00:08:02.122
This is my family.

00:08:02.784 --> 00:08:05.580
And in my family,

00:08:05.604 --> 00:08:09.823
there is one other person
who is also autistic.

00:08:10.411 --> 00:08:11.688
My mother.

00:08:11.712 --> 00:08:15.348
Yes, adult women can also be autistic.

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:20.261
My dad and my brother
are both nonautistic.

00:08:20.912 --> 00:08:25.212
Sometimes it can be a bit difficult
for us to communicate with each other,

00:08:25.236 --> 00:08:26.386
however.

00:08:27.045 --> 00:08:29.831
Sometimes I'll say something like,

00:08:29.855 --> 00:08:32.826
"Oh, Toronto's Union Station, right?"

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:36.572
thinking that I can

00:08:36.596 --> 00:08:39.683
help them to remember
certain aspects of it.

00:08:39.707 --> 00:08:44.617
When they get confused, I often
have to elaborate myself on that.

00:08:46.284 --> 00:08:49.807
And we often have to say things

00:08:49.831 --> 00:08:53.413
in a number of different ways
so that everyone understands.

00:08:54.419 --> 00:08:55.862
However, despite all that,

00:08:55.886 --> 00:08:59.686
we all love each other
and respect each other as equals.

00:09:00.188 --> 00:09:02.545
In his book "NeuroTribes,"

00:09:02.569 --> 00:09:08.786
author Steve Silberman states
that autism and other mental conditions

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:11.730
should be seen as naturally human,

00:09:11.754 --> 00:09:14.609
naturally part of a human spectrum

00:09:14.633 --> 00:09:16.577
and not as defects.

00:09:16.601 --> 00:09:19.910
And this is something
that I agree to completely.

00:09:20.903 --> 00:09:25.450
If autism was seen as part
of a natural human spectrum,

00:09:25.474 --> 00:09:32.069
then the world could be designed
to work better for autistic people.

00:09:32.728 --> 00:09:35.450
I am not ashamed of my autism.

00:09:36.188 --> 00:09:39.188
And I may not think like you,

00:09:39.212 --> 00:09:40.887
or act like you,

00:09:40.911 --> 00:09:44.124
but I am still human
and I am not diseased.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:46.889
Thank you.

00:09:46.913 --> 00:09:51.008
(Applause)

